course: euphemisms
lesson: 22
title: Soft words for hard things: work, firing and the economy and business and advertising
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Job titles and adverts are full of soft words, because people want their work to sound grand and sellers want their goods to sound better than they are.
explain: Some are jokes ("domestic engineer" for someone who runs the home), some are polite, and many are sales spin.
explain: Estate agents (the people who sell houses and flats) are famous for it: a flat described as "bijou" and "deceptively spacious" is almost certainly tiny.
explain: Shops do it too: a "pre-owned" car is simply a used car, and a "price adjustment" is nearly always a price rise.
explain: The trick is to turn each soft word back into the plain one and see what is left.
fact: "Domestic engineer" is a joking name for a housewife, househusband or homemaker.
fact: "Associate" often means an ordinary, low-paid employee, especially in US shops.
fact: "Underemployed" means working fewer hours than you want, or in a job below your skills.
fact: "Jobseeker" means an unemployed person (UK official language).
fact: "Economically inactive" means not working and not looking for work (UK official language).
fact: "On the bench" means a consultant (a hired expert who works on projects for other firms) has no project to work on.
fact: "A career-limiting move" is a joking name for a mistake that could get you fired.
fact: "Efficiency gains" often means fewer staff doing the same work.
fact: "Pre-owned" means used.
fact: "Pre-loved" means second-hand (mostly UK).
fact: "Vintage" can simply mean old and used.
fact: "Fun-size" means small, as in a small chocolate bar.
fact: "Cosy" in a house advert means very small.
fact: "Compact" means small.
fact: "Bijou" in a house advert means tiny (mostly UK).
fact: "Deceptively spacious" in a house advert usually means small (mostly UK).
fact: "Up-and-coming area" usually means a poor or run-down neighbourhood.
fact: "Needs some TLC" ("tender loving care") means a place is in bad condition and needs repairs.
fact: "Full of character" in a house advert often means old and needing repairs.
fact: "Price adjustment" usually means a price increase.
exercise: What does "domestic engineer" mean when someone says it with a smile? | A homemaker who runs the house | An engineer who builds houses | A mechanic who fixes washing machines | A worker at a power station
exercise: What does "associate" usually mean on a US shop worker's badge? | An ordinary, low-paid employee | A partner who owns part of the shop | A friend of the manager | A visiting expert from head office
exercise: What does it mean to be "underemployed"? | To work fewer hours or below your skills than you want | To have no job at all and be looking for one every day | To work too many hours a week | To work underground in a mine
exercise: Who is a "jobseeker" in UK official language? | An unemployed person | A person who hires new workers | A detective looking for clues | A worker who changes jobs often
exercise: What does "economically inactive" mean in UK statistics? | Not working and not looking for work | Working but earning very little | Spending no money at all | Too lazy to do any chores
exercise: What does it mean if a consultant is "on the bench"? | He has no project to work on | He is sitting in the park at lunch | He is a judge in a court | He is a reserve player in a team
exercise: What is a "career-limiting move"? | A mistake that could get you fired | A move to a smaller office | A sensible step up the ladder | Taking a job far from home
exercise: When a company boasts of "efficiency gains", what does it often mean? | Fewer staff doing the same work | Faster computers for every worker | Lower electricity bills | More breaks for the staff
exercise: What does "pre-owned" mean on a car advert? | Used | Owned before it was made | Brand new and never driven | Booked
exercise: What does "pre-loved" mean in a UK shop? | Second-hand | Very popular with buyers | Handmade with care | Given as a gift
exercise: What can "vintage" really mean on a cheap clothes stall? | Old and used | Made of very fine wine | Rare and very valuable | Made last week in an old style
exercise: What does "fun-size" mean on a chocolate bar? | Small | Extra large | With a toy inside | Funny
exercise: In a house advert, what does "cosy" usually mean? | Very small | Very warm and well heated | Very quiet and peaceful | Very soft and comfortable
exercise: What does "compact" mean in a car or flat advert? | Small | Strong and well built | Packed full of furniture | Cheap
exercise: In a UK property advert, what does "bijou" mean? | Tiny | Covered in jewels | French and very stylish | Grand
exercise: What does "deceptively spacious" usually mean in a house advert? | The place is small | The place is huge inside | The place has a hidden room | The place has very high ceilings
exercise: What does an estate agent usually mean by an "up-and-coming area"? | A poor or run-down neighbourhood | A rich area on a hill | An area full of new skyscrapers | A place where famous people live
exercise: What does "needs some TLC" mean in a house advert? | It is in bad condition and needs repairs | It needs a family that loves pets | It needs a fresh coat of polish | It needs new curtains and cushions
exercise: What does "full of character" often mean in a house advert? | Old and needing repairs | Full of interesting people | Decorated with cartoon figures | Brand new and modern
exercise: What does a shop usually mean by a "price adjustment"? | A price increase | A price cut in a sale | A fix to a printing mistake | A free gift with each item
test: Estate agent's advert: "Bijou flat, deceptively spacious, in an up-and-coming area." What are you most likely to find? | A tiny flat in a run-down neighbourhood | A huge flat in a rich neighbourhood | A flat full of jewels | A new flat on a building site
test: "aw it's fun-size, can i have 4 then" — what does the speaker know about the bar? | It is small | It is a joke bar | It has a toy inside | It is extra big
test: A letter from your phone company: "From June there will be a small price adjustment." What is going to happen? | You will pay more | You will pay less | Nothing will change | Your phone will be fixed
test: "crap, told the boss his plan was rubbish in front of everyone. career-limiting move, eh" — what does the speaker fear? | Losing the job or a promotion | Moving to a smaller desk | Being made the boss | Getting a medal for honesty
test: Lily says: "a pre-owned car means nobody has owned it yet." Is she right? | No, it has had an owner before | Yes, it is brand new | Yes, it is still in the factory | No, it has never been driven
test: Gran says: "I'm not a housewife, I'm a domestic engineer!" What is she doing? | Joking about the work of running a home | Saying she builds houses | Saying she fixes cars | Saying she works at the power plant
test: A report says: "Many graduates (people who have finished university) are underemployed." What does it mean? | They work fewer hours or in jobs below their skills | They have far too much work and no time to rest | They earn too much money | They have no degrees
test: House advert: "Full of character, needs some TLC." What should a buyer expect? | An old house needing repairs | A brand new house | A house with famous owners | A house full of toys
test: The boss announces "efficiency gains" next year. What should the staff worry about? | Some jobs may be cut | Free fruit in the kitchen | Faster lifts | New chairs
test: News: "The number of jobseekers rose by 3,000 last month." What happened? | More people were unemployed | More companies were hiring | More people found jobs | More people retired
test: "she's on the bench again, no client since May" — what is true of her? | She has no project to work on | She is sitting in a park | She plays football | She is a judge
test: A charity shop sign: "Pre-loved toys, all £1." What are the toys? | Second-hand | Brand new | Handmade | Broken ones
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the descriptor "deceptively spacious" as employed in the particulars of a residential property. | The accommodation is of limited dimensions | The accommodation is of exceptional dimensions | The accommodation contains concealed chambers | The accommodation possesses elevated ceilings
exam: It is given that a retailer announces a "price adjustment" in respect of its goods. Select the response most consistent with customary usage. | The prices are to be increased | The prices are to be reduced | The prices are to remain unaltered | The goods are to be withdrawn from sale
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a person classified as "economically inactive"? | He is neither employed nor seeking employment | He is employed upon a low wage | He abstains from all expenditure | He is employed upon a part-time basis
exam: Ascertain the plain meaning of the term "pre-owned" when applied to a motor vehicle offered for sale. | Previously used by another owner | Reserved in advance for a purchaser | Manufactured prior to the current year | Never previously driven
